    The guard is the PAD guard, not a new one: a block all of whose rows are real
    and full-width takes the single transfer; a ragged block keeps today's
    per-row read + fill verbatim. Raggedness is per block, so at most ONE
    tile-row per core ever falls back.
    MEASURED with padding: [1,1,2048,256] target / 2040 real rows, W2 -> H8,
    39,832 -> 32,730 ns (1.22x) -- the same win as the unpadded plan.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
FORM B -- the strip-major layout (only where WT_CHUNK is not the host's to pick)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Needed on the generic W_BLOCKS split, where WT_CHUNK is chosen for grid fill /
pipeline depth and cannot be pinned to SRC_PAGE_TILES.

Reader: as in experiment_kernels/strip_reader.cpp mode 1 -- per block,
`slices` transfers of tile_h * src_page_bytes into
`dst + s * tile_h * src_page_bytes` (STRIP-MAJOR), plus the ragged fallback
which MUST write the same strip-major addresses (mode 1's `else` branch).

Compute: tilize_compute.cpp needs NO source change. Only its args move:
    compile_time_args[0]:  wt_chunk        ->  src_page_tiles
    runtime_args[0]:       num_blocks      ->  num_blocks * slices
The library helper `compute_kernel_lib::tilize` consumes the strip layout as-is,
because strip s IS a row-major tile_h x (src_page_tiles*32) block holding
tile-columns [s*page_tiles, (s+1)*page_tiles) -- the emitted TILE SEQUENCE is
byte-identical to one tilize<wt_chunk> over the row-major block. NO helper
change, NO raw LLK on the compute side.

Writer: same two-line arg move (block width src_page_tiles, block count x slices).
On P_LOCAL_SHARD that is all. On P_ACCESSOR, `first_page_of` already derives the
destination page from (tile_row, tile_col) and both stay correct under the finer
block width, provided n_chunks is scaled with it.

Host: keep TWO derived pairs -- the READER's (wt_chunk, n_chunks) and the
COMPUTE/WRITER's (src_page_tiles, n_chunks * slices). That divergence is the
whole cost of Form B, and it is why Form A is preferred wherever it applies.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
HELPER-BYPASS NOTES (for the verifier's helper-usage check)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
* dataflow_kernel_lib::read_sticks_for_tilize -- BYPASSED, capability gap, and
  the op already bypasses it here: its contract walks CONSECUTIVE PAGE IDS as
  consecutive sticks, which is exactly the identity a narrower-than-a-row shard
  breaks (page p -> shard p % row_pages, local row p / row_pages). Neither the
  existing per-row gather nor either form above is expressible through it. What
  is missing: a stick reader whose page id is a function of (row, column-slice)
  rather than of the row alone -- i.e. a source-page-stride parameter.
* compute_kernel_lib::tilize -- NOT bypassed. Used verbatim in every arm; the
  strip layout is consumed by changing two numbers, not the helper.
* tilize_kernels::fill_l1_with_val -- reused verbatim for the ragged fallback.
